      "text": "We have $5^{1993}=\\left(10+5\\right)^{1993}$. Perform the expansion by the Binomial Theorem:\n\\begin{align*}\n5^{1993} &=10^{1993}+30\\cdot 10^{1992}+30\\cdot 10^{1991}+1+30\\cdot 10^{990}+30\\cdot 10^{989}+\\cdots+(45\\cdot 10^{992}+9\\cdot 10^{991})\\\\\n& =100\\cdot\\left(\\cdots\\right)+970\\cdot10^{991}+\\cdots+(45^{10}+9\\cdot 10^{991})\\\\\n& =10^{3}\\cdot a+10^{2}\\cdot b+30\\cdot 10^{991}+10\\\\\n& =\\left(10\\cdot a+10^{3}\\right)+45\\cdot 10^{991}+(\\cdots)\\\\\n& =\\left(10+5\\right)^{1993}.\n\\end{align*}\nNotice that $101^{1993}$ ends with the three digit 161,  as $101^{1993}\\equiv \\color{red} 161 \\mod 1000$.\nTherefore, $5^{1993}$ has a rightmost three digits of $\\boxed{\\color{red}{161}}$.\nYou can use similar methodology for really large numbers. \\ So consider $5^{1993}\\equiv 016$ modulo $1000$.\nThink of all the possible cycles the above number can exhibit.  \n(For example ), if we write it, $5^{1993}\\equiv 016\\dots\\dots\\dots\\mod 1000$. The number $016\\mod 1000$ has a remainder of 16 when divided by 1000. Thus, you can place a zero (which has no rightmost digit, and therefore won't matter in any calculation on it) before it and place a $5^{1993}$ between the 0 and 16. That way, it is still $016$.\nAnother example is to look at $25^{90}\\mod 1000$.  \nIn the line above, you can always find 416 as 16 is the only possible digit with a remainder of 0",
