      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of $5^{1993}$, we need to find the value of $5^{1993} \\mod 1000$. This means we need to find the last three digits of $5^{1993}$.\n\nAs $5$ is a prime number, we can use the special property of $5$ to simplify the calculation. The cyclicity property of $5$ states that the last digit of powers of $5$ only ends up with $5, 25, 125, 625, 3125, ...$ (i.e. $5, 5, 5, ...$).\n\nThis means that the last three digits of each power of $5$ will have a cycle of 4. That is, for $k$ being an integer, $5^k \\mod 1000$ will have the following relationship with $k$:\n- When $k=0$, $5^k = 1$, giving last three digits as `001`.\n- When $k=1$, $5^0 = 1$, giving last three digits as `001`.\n- When $k=2$, $5^2 = 25$, giving last three digits as `025`.\n- When $k=3$, $5^3 = 125$, giving last three digits as `125`\n- When $k=0$, $5^k = 1$, giving last three digits as `001`\n- When $k=4$, $5^4 = 625$, giving last three digits as `625`\n- The pattern continues.\n\nNow we just need to find how many cycles fit in $1992$ with remainder $1$ by using this relationship.\n\n$1992\\mod 4= 0$, thus the cyclic identity stops at $k=1992$. Leaving us with $1$, so $1993 \\mod 4$ has a remainder of $1$, leading us to our last term of the cyclical group $='1'$. Thus, $1993\\mod 4=1$, having the last digit `\"1\"` in its cycle.\n\nDeveloping this idea into a function:\n- Find all the multiples of 4 from $1993 \\mod 4$ by which $k$ meets to get exactly the value $k=1993\\mod 4.$\n- Determine the remainder after which you get exactly the returned value, i.e. $'1'.$\n\nHere is Python code to solve this problem. Note that due to Python's integer precision, we may run into issues with higher powers still going beyond `1000`.\n\n```python\ndef power_of_5_modulo(base, exponent, modulus):\n    base_mod = base % modulus\n    result = pow(base_mod, exponent, modulus)\n    return result\n\n# Solution:\nprint(power_of_5_modulo(5, 1993, 1000))\n```\nWhen run, this code will provide a reasonable approximation of $5^{1993} \\mod 1000$. However, due to computational limitations, such a method may not be ideal for very large exponents.\n\nIn practical terms, this problem can be augmented by various mathematical approaches and algorithms, such as repeated squaring for speed, or an efficient exponentiation using Fourier transform, which makes computations with large exponents more feasible. This step is usually done in computational systems ranging from academic research-level hardware (like GPUs or Tensilica Xtensa architectures) to real-world commercial systems.\n\nIn summary, while programmatically calculating this problem's solution involves diving straight into calculations or using straightforward modular arithmetic principles, employing more advanced computational techniques can provide an optimal time and space efficiency approach.",
